<section class="content-section">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Converting IDT to EST</h2>
  <div class="use-cases">
    <article>
      <h3>1. Forgetting the Sunday–Thursday Workweek</h3>
      <p>
        Israel operates on a Sunday-to-Thursday workweek. Scheduling Friday meetings in Tel Aviv often fails because Friday is part of the Israeli weekend.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>2. Confusing IDT with IST</h3>
      <p>
        IST is Israel Standard Time (UTC+2) used in winter, while IDT is Israel Daylight Time (UTC+3) used in summer. Using the wrong offset creates 1-hour errors.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>3. Date Boundary Errors</h3>
      <p>
        Converting IDT times between 12:00 AM and 7:59 AM shifts the EST time back into the previous calendar evening.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

